<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander von Humboldt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander von Humboldt was a Prussian naturalist, geographer and explorer. From 1799 to 1804 he travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> with the botanist Aimé Bonpland, and his Political Essay on the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s population, mines and economy. His work helped found modern physical geography, and his multi-volume Kosmos sought to describe the unity of nature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt.jpg" alt="Alexander von Humboldt" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Karl Stieler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stieler,_Joseph_Karl_-_Alexander_von_Humboldt_-_1843.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Friedrich Wilhelm Heinrich Alexander von Humboldt was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 14 September 1769 and baptised a Lutheran. He was the younger brother of the Prussian minister and linguist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-von-humboldt">Wilhelm von Humboldt</a>. He graduated from the Freiberg School of Mines in 1792 and became an inspector in the Prussian Department of Mines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> and the Fichtel Mountains. His gold ore output in his first year exceeded that of the previous eight years combined. He also opened, at his own expense, a free school for miners and tried to set up an emergency relief fund for them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Madrid Humboldt obtained authorisation from the Spanish crown to travel in its American realms, after a planned French voyage was postponed. With the botanist Aimé Bonpland he sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">La Coruña</a> on 5 June 1799, climbed the volcano Teide on Tenerife, and landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumana">Cumaná</a>, Venezuela, on 16 July.</p><p>Between 1799 and 1804 they travelled through Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, the Andes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, José Celestino Mutis gave Humboldt access to his great pictorial record of plants. Humboldt identified the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco River</a>, and in 1802 he climbed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> to 19,286 feet, a world record altitude for a Westerner at the time. In 1804 he made an unplanned visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at the invitation of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>.</p><p>Humboldt spent his personal fortune publishing the results, in nearly thirty volumes with Bonpland as co-author. The Political Essay on the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and the Personal Narrative of Travels were widely read. He was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Prussian Academy of Sciences</a> in 1805 and, in his sixtieth year, made an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His quantitative work on botanical geography laid the foundation of biogeography, and his advocacy of long-term, systematic geophysical measurement pioneered modern geomagnetic and meteorological monitoring. His multivolume Kosmos, which took shape in lectures at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a> in 1827–1828, sought to unify diverse branches of scientific knowledge and presented the universe as one interacting whole. In 1800 and again in 1831 he described local effects of development on climate. He died on 6 May 1859. Scholars argue over whether his writing carries an imperial bias; Pratt contends that he portrayed the South American natural world as free of people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander von Humboldt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Spain reached its peak of wealth around 1800, when it had some six million inhabitants, its mines were producing more silver than ever, and <b>Alexander von Humboldt</b> described Mexico City as one of the finest cities built by Europeans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alexander von Humboldt</b> published pages 47, 48 and 50 to 52 in his 1810 atlas Vues des Cordillères et Monuments des Peuples Indigènes de l'Amérique, the first reproduction of any of its pages.</blockquote><a href="/work/dresden-codex">Dresden Codex · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alexander von Humboldt</b> studied it in 1801–1802.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-4">4 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1827" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alexander von Humboldt"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5690 5568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5690 5568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1827">What was here in 1827? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-journeys"><p class="enc-article-foot enc-journey"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/journeys?j=humboldt">On the globe, stop by stop: Humboldt and Bonpland in the Spanish Americas &rarr;</a></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Slavery is no doubt the greatest evil that afflicts human nature, whether we consider the slave torn from his family in his native country and thrown into the hold of a slave ship…&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Narrative, chapter 28, on Cuba &#183; tr. Thomasina Ross, 1852-53 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7254/pg7254-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If a traveller may be permitted to speak of his personal emotions, I shall add, that on that night I experienced the realization of one of the dreams of my early youth.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Narrative, chapter 2, on first seeing the Southern Cross, July 1799 &#183; tr. Thomasina Ross, 1852-53 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6322/pg6322-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the solitude of the seas we hail a star as a friend, from whom we have long been separated.&#8221;</p><footer>Personal Narrative, chapter 2 &#183; tr. Thomasina Ross, 1852-53 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6322/pg6322-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;While we maintain the unity of the human species, we at the same time repel the depressing assumption of superior and inferior races of men.&#8221;</p><footer>Cosmos, vol. 1 (1845) &#183; tr. E. C. Otté, 1849 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14565/pg14565-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There are nations more susceptible of cultivation, more highly civilized, more enobled by mental cultivation than others, but none in themselves nobler than others. All are in like degree designed for freedom…&#8221;</p><footer>Cosmos, vol. 1 (1845) &#183; tr. E. C. Otté, 1849 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14565/pg14565-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nature considered 'rationally', that is to say, submitted to the process of thought, is a unity in diversity of phenomena; a harmony blending together all created things, however dissimilar in form and attributes…&#8221;</p><footer>Cosmos, vol. 1 (1845), introduction &#183; tr. E. C. Otté, 1849 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14565/pg14565-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
